#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# dimensions

#% text
# `dimensions(x)` gives the list of the lengths of the levels of `x` that form a full array: `[2, 3]` for a 2 by 3 matrix.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Each level counts while all its parts have one head and one length; a ragged level ends the list.

#% item
# An atom gives `[]`; any head works, not only `list`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
dimensions([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]])
#% output
#= [2, 3]

#% cell
dimensions(range(5))
#% output
#= [5]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Ragged lists stop at the first uneven level:

#% cell
dimensions([[1, 2], [3]])
#% output
#= [2]

#% text
# Other heads:

#% cell
dimensions(f(g(a, b), g(c, d)))
#% output
#= [2, 2]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The length of `dimensions` is `array_depth`:

#% cell
m = constant_array(0, [2, 3, 4])
[dimensions(m), array_depth(m)]
#% output
#= [[2, 3, 4], 3]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `array_depth`, `length`, `depth`, `constant_array`
